# Vulnerability details

## Impact
When users try to add liquidity via permit2 way, and the transaction is reverted because of some reasons, for example, slippage control, malicious users can get users' unused signature and transfer users' funds via permit2.

## Proof of Concept
In sea water, we allow users to add liquidity via permit2. Users can sign one signature and add liquidity with one valid signature. Considering seawater will be deployed in Arbitrum, malicious users hardly get this valid permit2 signature before this transaction is executed. When this transaction is executed successfully, the signature cannot be reused because of the replay protection.

The problem is that if this transaction is not executed successfully or re-org happens, malicious users can get the valid signature via reverted transaction or re-org transactions. Then malicious users can steal users' funds from permit2 via this valid signature.

In Arbitrum, re-org behavior is common. And adding liquidity transaction is reverted is quite possible considering that LP holders may add some slippage control when they add liquidity.

